You have authenticated X/Twitter API access. I have one night of it. Pull as
much of my account's record as the API will give you, write it to files as you
go, and tell me exactly what you could not get.

ACCOUNT
  handle    @danfrank
  user id   16430736
  public    yes (not protected)
  bio       "insufferable politics sicko"

WHAT ALREADY EXISTS, SO YOU KNOW WHAT IS NEW
  A local archive holds 2,741 of my posts, 2008-09-24 through 2026-09-01, from
  three sources of unequal fidelity: 1,412 rows from a spreadsheet export,
  1,098 from a browser scrape, 231 from a 10-results-per-query search backend.
  It is originals-only by its own inclusion rule. It has almost no replies,
  five retweets, five media rows, no expanded links, no client/source field, no
  geo, no likes-given, and no social graph. Every one of those is a hole this
  session can fill.

===============================================================================
THE FOUR RULES
===============================================================================

1. NEVER INVENT A ROW. A file that is empty or explicitly a lower bound is a
   correct result. A file that looks complete and is not is the only outcome
   here worse than doing nothing. If a query caps at N results and returns N,
   say so per window — that window is truncated, not exhausted.

2. EXCLUSION DIRECTIVE, non-negotiable. Drop, do not file, any post that
   mentions @Lo_weez, "Annie", or "Ulmer" in any form — the post text, a reply
   parent, a quoted tweet, a like target, a follow row, anything. Do not
   summarise them, do not count them into a per-month total, do not describe
   what they were about. Report only one number: excluded_under_directive: <n>.
   There are ~17 known such posts spanning 2015-2020. This is a safety
   directive about a living person, not an editorial preference.

3. NOTHING PRIVATE GOES IN A FILE. These files land in a public repository whose
   history cannot be un-published. So: no DM content or DM metadata, no email
   addresses, no phone numbers, no login/IP/device history, no ad-targeting or
   audience data, no API keys or bearer tokens, no other person's private data.
   If a payload contains any of it, strip the field and name the field you
   stripped rather than filing it and telling me after.

4. WRITE AS YOU GO, ONE FILE PER DELIVERABLE. Do not hold results in context and
   write at the end — if the session dies, everything unwritten is gone. Finish
   a deliverable, write its file, note it in the manifest, start the next.

===============================================================================
OUTPUT FORMAT
===============================================================================

JSONL, one object per line, UTF-8. Timestamps as UTC "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS" with
no suffix. Every id as a STRING, never a number — they exceed 2^53 and JSON
parsers silently corrupt them.

Post rows use this schema. It is a superset of what I already hold, so a merge
is a dedup by `id` and nothing else:

  {
    "id": "932618140",
    "created_at": "2008-09-24T04:52:36",
    "type": "original" | "reply" | "quote" | "repost",
    "text": "<full untruncated text, note_tweet expanded if over 280>",
    "lang": "en",
    "likes": 0, "replies": 0, "reposts": 0,
    "quotes": 0, "bookmarks": null, "impressions": null,
    "url": "https://x.com/danfrank/status/932618140",
    "source": "api-2026-09-03",
    "client": "Twitter for iPhone",
    "conversation_id": "932618140",
    "in_reply_to_status_id": null,
    "in_reply_to_user_id": null,
    "in_reply_to_screen_name": null,
    "quoted": {"id": "...", "handle": "...", "text": "..."} | null,
    "retweeted": {"id": "...", "handle": "...", "text": "..."} | null,
    "media": [{"type":"photo|video|gif","url":"...","alt_text":"...",
               "duration_ms":null,"width":0,"height":0}],
    "urls": [{"short":"https://t.co/xyz","expanded":"https://...",
              "title":"...","description":"..."}],
    "hashtags": [], "mentions": [{"id":"...","handle":"..."}],
    "geo": {"place_id":"...","name":"...","full_name":"...",
            "country":"...","coordinates":[lon,lat]} | null,
    "context_annotations": [{"domain":"...","entity":"..."}],
    "possibly_sensitive": false,
    "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["..."],
    "is_self_thread": false
  }

Fields the API does not return are `null`. Never a guessed 0 — a real zero and
an unknown are different facts and I need to be able to tell them apart. Extra
keys are welcome; drop nothing because I did not list it.

===============================================================================
DELIVERABLES, IN PRIORITY ORDER
===============================================================================

Work top-down. If the night ends at item 6, that is a good night.

--- TIER 0 — three numbers that settle live claims (do these first, minutes) ---

  01-account.json
  The user object, every field the API exposes.
  THE ONE FIELD THAT MATTERS MOST: `created_at`. My wiki currently dates the
  account to September 2008 by inference from a #MyTwitterAnniversary post
  reading "14 years", posted 2022-09-24. That is an inference, not a
  timestamp, and it is cited on a page. Your `created_at` either confirms it
  or corrects it.
  Also: public_metrics.tweet_count — this bounds how far my 2,741 rows are
  from the truth, which nothing currently does; followers_count (256 as of
  June 2026), following_count, listed_count; verified_type; profile_image_url;
  pinned_tweet_id; the `location` and `url` profile fields; `protected`;
  `withheld` if present.
  If screen-name history, display-name history, or bio history is reachable at
  all, take it — I have exactly one bio string on record and no history.

--- TIER 1 — completeness (the point of the night) -----------------------------

  02-timeline-full.jsonl
  EVERY post on the account, all types, oldest to newest, no window skipped:
  originals, replies, quote tweets, and pure retweets. Page to exhaustion.
  Report the per-page cap you hit and the total pages walked.

  These windows are known-thin or known-empty locally. If your pull covers
  them, say so explicitly per window, because "the API returned nothing here"
  and "I never asked" have to be distinguishable in the manifest:

    2008-10 .. 2009-10-19   every month is a truncated 10-result window; the
                            true counts are unknown and higher than mine
    2009-01-01 .. 2013-08-16  no spreadsheet coverage; scrape only
    2015-04, 2015-08..11    zero rows locally (141-day hole Jul->Dec 2015)
    2018-07, 2018-08, 2018-11
    2019-02, 2019-06, 2019-07
    2020-06, 2020-08, 2020-09, 2020-11, 2020-12
    2021-02 .. 2021-09      a 273-day hole, 2021-01-25 -> 2021-10-26
    2025-03 .. 2026-02      eleven of twelve months empty; ONE row in 2025-10
    2026-04-07 .. 2026-05-30   52-day hole between export sources
    2026-06-14 .. 2026-08-07   54-day hole; 2026-07 entirely empty

  2025 MATTERS MORE THAN THE REST. My wiki calls 2025 "the collapse-year
  silence: 13 originals, the lowest count in the archive" and reasons from it
  on other pages. If those months are actually populated and my export simply
  missed them, a published finding is wrong and I need to know tonight. Give
  me the true 2025 count as its own line in the manifest whatever it is.

  03-counts-by-month.csv
  month,posts,originals,replies,quotes,retweets — every month from account
  creation to today, zeros included. This is the completeness check: it lets me
  diff your census against mine month by month instead of trusting either.

--- TIER 2 — fields my archive does not have at all ----------------------------

  Fold these into 02 if your endpoint returns them inline. Only make separate
  files for what needs a second pass.

  04-urls-expanded.jsonl
  The expanded target of every shortened link I ever posted: 151 t.co, 25
  twitpic, 24 bit.ly, 15 4sq.com (Foursquare check-ins), 10 yfrog, 3 tinyurl.
  Right now a link in my archive is evidence that I shared something and no
  record of what. Expand every one the API resolves. Dead targets: record the
  expanded URL anyway and mark `resolved: false` — a dead nytimes.com/2010/...
  URL still tells me what I read.

  05-clients.jsonl
  The `source` / client-app string per post, if the API still exposes it. Over
  seventeen years this is a device timeline — Twitter for iPhone vs web vs
  Tweetdeck vs Instagram vs Foursquare — and it cross-checks a separate
  analysis I have of how my writing changes by mode. Nothing in my corpus
  currently records what I posted *from*.

  06-geo.jsonl
  Every post with place or coordinate data. My only street-level location
  evidence before 2014 is fifteen Foursquare check-ins in 2010-2011 recovered
  from link text. Real geo payloads on those, and on anything else geotagged,
  would put dates on two apartment moves that are currently inferred.

  07-media.jsonl
  Every media attachment ever posted: type, direct URL, alt text, dimensions,
  duration, view count where available. I have five media rows total. Include
  media on deleted-parent replies if reachable. Do NOT download the files —
  URLs only.

  08-repairs.jsonl
  125 rows in my archive have text truncated where a link was cut (114 end in
  the "…" glyph, 15 in three ASCII periods — different characters, and the
  distinction cost me a miscount once). If you have full-fidelity text for
  every post from item 02, this file is redundant; say so and skip it. If not,
  re-fetch the truncated ones and give me full text.

--- TIER 3 — the other side of the conversation --------------------------------

  My archive is 2,718 originals against 22 replies. That ratio is an artifact
  of how it was built, not of how I use the site, and it means the corpus has
  my monologue and none of my dialogue.

  09-replies-with-parents.jsonl
  Every reply I ever posted, each row carrying the PARENT tweet hydrated:
  parent id, author handle and id, parent text, parent created_at. A reply
  without what it replied to is unreadable. Where the parent is deleted or
  protected, keep my reply and mark `parent_available: false`.

  10-threads.jsonl
  Every conversation_id where I posted two or more times, reassembled in order
  — my own threads, and threads I participated in. One named target: the
  thread rooted at 1548831640528510976. My archive has part 1 of three and I
  have been looking for parts 2 and 3 for months.

  11-quotes.jsonl
  Every quote tweet I posted with the quoted tweet's full text, author and
  date. I have 12 rows with a `quoted` field and most carry a summary rather
  than the actual text.

--- TIER 4 — amplification and attention (entirely absent locally) --------------

  12-retweets.jsonl
  Every pure retweet, all seventeen years. My archive has five, and excludes
  the rest by construction — which means every "quiet year" I have written down
  means quiet in things I wrote, not quiet on the site. This one probably
  changes a published conclusion.

  13-likes.jsonl
  Every post I have ever liked, with liked-at timestamp if the API gives one,
  and the target post's id, author, date and text. This is the single richest
  thing available tonight and I have none of it: a seventeen-year record of
  what held my attention, including everything I read and never responded to.
  If the endpoint caps or only returns recent likes, take what it gives and
  report the cap and the earliest liked-at you reached.

  14-bookmarks.jsonl
  Same shape. Deliberate saves rather than reflexive likes — a smaller and
  probably sharper signal.

